# sum-refined.bot -- tail-style recursion over an accumulator whose type is
# only known through an integer? refinement, plus a closure created per
# step.
#
# Canonical surface-Botlish source (CANONICAL-SURFACE-BENCHMARKS.md);
# migrated from the historical raw-core-IR fixture bench/sum-refined.ir,
# which remains in bench/ only as a frozen input for historical audit-
# reproduction tooling (see that report) -- it is no longer canonical
# benchmark source.
#
# The old raw IR's "else" branch used a bare `error-value` core-IR op to
# assert unreachability (acc is always an Int on every real call in this
# benchmark's own domain -- it starts at 0 and every step adds an Int).
# Surface Botlish has no way to construct an error/Result value from source
# at all (examples/stdlib/hashtable.bot's own header note: "Botlish has no
# way to construct a Result value from surface syntax today"), so that
# exact op cannot be transliterated. This keeps the same provably-dead
# refinement-check structure (the compiler still has to analyze both
# branches, which is the point) and returns a sentinel Int in the
# unreachable branch instead of inventing new error-raising syntax to
# rescue the original shape (forbidden by this migration's own rules).
#
# expect: 80200

fn refined_sum(n, acc):
    if integer?(acc):
        if n == 0:
            return acc
        fn step(x):
            x + n
        refined_sum(n - 1, step(acc))
    else:
        -1

refined_sum(400, 0)
